{"skill_id":"step-resp","version":4,"questions":[{"id":"step-resp-r1-q4","version":1,"round":1,"type":"order","prompt":"Order the link from an impulse response to a step-response specification.","explanation":"The input identity comes first, and linearity carries it over to the output. The lower limit is 0 only after causality is used; a two-sided impulse response needs the integral from the distant past. Rise and settling describe the step waveform. Reading them off the peak of the impulse response confuses the kick with the accumulated response.","steps":["A unit step is the running integral of a unit impulse.","By linearity, the step response is the running integral of the impulse response.","For a causal impulse response, start that integral at time 0.","Read rise and settling from that integral, not from the height of the impulse response."]},{"id":"step-resp-r2-q4","version":1,"round":2,"type":"symbolic","prompt":"A causal impulse response is \\(h(t) = a e^{-b t}\\) for \\(t \\ge 0\\), with \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) positive. Write the unit-step response for \\(t \\ge 0\\), using the names \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(t\\).","answer":"(a/b)*(1-exp(-b*t))","tolerance":0.001,"explanation":"The unit-step response is the running integral of \\(h\\). Integrating \\(a e^{-b\\tau}\\) from 0 to \\(t\\) produces \\((a/b)(1-e^{-bt})\\). The factor \\(a/b\\) is the final value, the whole area under \\(h\\). Leaving out \\(1/b\\) treats the exponent as if its coefficient were 1. The expression \\(a e^{-bt}\\) is still the impulse response, not the step.","variables":[{"name":"a","min":0.4,"max":6.0},{"name":"b","min":0.3,"max":4.0},{"name":"t","min":0.05,"max":3.0}],"alternates":["a*(1-exp(-b*t))/b","a/b-a*exp(-b*t)/b"]},{"id":"step-resp-r3-q1","round":3,"type":"numeric","prompt":"A first-order system with unit DC gain and \\(\\tau=0.5\\ \\mathrm{s}\\) starts at rest. Its input is a pulse of height 2 from \\(t=0\\) to \\(t=1\\ \\mathrm{s}\\), then 0. What is the output at \\(t=1.5\\ \\mathrm{s}\\)?","answer":0.636185,"unit":"","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"While the pulse is on, \\(y\\) rises toward 2: \\(y(1)=2\\left(1-e^{-2}\\right)=1.729\\). After the input turns off, y decays from that value toward 0: \\(y(1.5)=1.729\\,e^{-0.5/0.5}=1.729\\times0.368=0.636\\).\n• \\(2e^{-1}=0.74\\) assumes y had already reached 2 when the pulse ended.\n• Using \\(e^{-1.5/0.5}\\) measures the decay from \\(t=0\\) instead of from the moment the input switched off.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Input: a pulse of height 2 from t = 0 to t = 1 s, then zero.\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" style=\"max-width:340px;width:100%;height:auto\"><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"176\"/><polygon points=\"330,176 323.6,178.7 323.6,173.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"16\"/><polygon points=\"50,16 52.7,22.4 47.3,22.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">t (s)</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">x(t)</text><polyline points=\"50,60.6 52.2,60.6 54.5,60.6 56.8,60.6 59,60.6 61.2,60.6 63.5,60.6 65.8,60.6 68,60.6 70.2,60.6 72.5,60.6 74.8,60.6 77,60.6 79.2,60.6 81.5,60.6 83.8,60.6 86,60.6 88.2,60.6 90.5,60.6 92.8,60.6 95,60.6 97.2,60.6 99.5,60.6 101.8,60.6 104,60.6 106.2,60.6 108.5,60.6 110.8,60.6 113,60.6 115.2,60.6 117.5,60.6 119.8,60.6 122,60.6 124.2,60.6 126.5,60.6 128.8,60.6 131,60.6 133.2,60.6 135.5,60.6 137.8,60.6 140,176 142.2,176 144.5,176 146.8,176 149,176 151.2,176 153.5,176 155.8,176 158,176 160.2,176 162.5,176 164.8,176 167,176 169.2,176 171.5,176 173.8,176 176,176 178.2,176 180.5,176 182.8,176 185,176 187.2,176 189.5,176 191.8,176 194,176 196.2,176 198.5,176 200.8,176 203,176 205.2,176 207.5,176 209.8,176 212,176 214.2,176 216.5,176 218.8,176 221,176 223.2,176 225.5,176 227.8,176 230,176 232.2,176 234.5,176 236.8,176 239,176 241.2,176 243.5,176 245.7,176 248,176 250.2,176 252.5,176 254.8,176 257,176 259.2,176 261.5,176 263.8,176 266,176 268.2,176 270.5,176 272.8,176 275,176 277.2,176 279.5,176 281.8,176 284,176 286.2,176 288.5,176 290.8,176 293,176 295.2,176 297.5,176 299.8,176 302,176 304.2,176 306.5,176 308.8,176 311,176 313.2,176 315.5,176 317.8,176 320,176\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg>","figure_alt":"Input: a pulse of height 2 from t = 0 to t = 1 s, then zero."},{"id":"step-resp-r1-q6","version":1,"round":1,"type":"mc","prompt":"For \\(t>0\\), which identity is the unit step?","options":["The running integral of a unit impulse from 0 to \\(t\\).","The derivative of a unit impulse.","The running integral of a unit step.","A pulse whose width equals its height."],"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A unit impulse has all of its area at one instant. Integrating that area from 0 to \\(t>0\\) yields 1, which is the unit step. Differentiating the impulse is not a step. Integrating the step produces a ramp. A pulse returns to zero, so it is not a step.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 176\" role=\"img\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\"><title>held pulse</title><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"318\" y2=\"146\"/><polygon points=\"318,146 311.6,148.7 311.6,143.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"46\" y1=\"146\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"24\"/><polygon points=\"46,24 48.7,30.4 43.3,30.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"318\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">t</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">y</text><polyline points=\"46,39 49,39 52,39 55.1,39 58.1,39 61.1,39 64.1,39 67.2,39 